(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-11-02"] = {"mth-11-02-m01":{"idea":"A locus is the path a point traces when it must follow a rule. This question asks for the meaning of the word.","steps":["A point moves, but it must always obey one condition.","All the positions it can take, put together, form a path.","That path is called the locus of the point.","Example: a point that stays 5 units from the origin traces the circle x² + y² = 25."],"others":"The distance from the origin is just one number, not a path. A count of points is not a path either. A single point cannot be a locus unless the condition allows only one position.","trap":"Locus means path of positions, not one position.","answer":"the path that it traces, when it moves under a given condition"},"mth-11-02-m02":{"idea":"A point that is the same distance from two fixed points lies on the line that cuts the segment between them in half at a right angle.","steps":["Let the fixed points be A and B, and let P be the moving point.","The condition is PA = PB.","Example: A(0, 0), B(4, 0). Then x² + y² = (x − 4)² + y².","This simplifies to 8x = 16, so x = 2, which is the perpendicular bisector of AB."],"others":"The mid-point is only one point, but many points are equidistant from A and B. The line through A and B holds points that are not equidistant. The circle with AB as diameter has points at different distances from A and B.","trap":"Equal distance from two points means perpendicular bisector.","answer":"the perpendicular bisector of the segment AB"},"mth-11-02-m03":{"idea":"The slope tells how steeply a line rises. It is the rise divided by the run.","steps":["Rise is the change in y: y₂ − y₁.","Run is the change in x: x₂ − x₁.","Slope m = rise/run = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁).","Check with (0, 0) and (1, 2): m = 2/1 = 2."],"others":"The sums y₂ + y₁ and x₂ + x₁ do not measure change. The version with x on top is the reciprocal, which is the slope of the line reflected over y = x. The product of changes is not a slope.","trap":"y goes on top, x at the bottom, and both are differences.","answer":"(y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁)"},"mth-11-02-m04":{"idea":"The slope is the tangent of the angle the line makes with the positive x axis.","steps":["m = tan θ, here θ = 135°.","135° = 180° − 45°.","tan(180° − 45°) = −tan 45°.","So m = −1."],"others":"1 is tan 45°, the slope for 45°. −√3 is tan 120°. 0 is the slope of a flat line, with θ = 0°.","trap":"An angle between 90° and 180° always gives a negative slope.","answer":"−1"},"mth-11-02-m05":{"idea":"Parallel lines point in the same direction. They make the same angle with the x axis.","steps":["Parallel lines have equal inclination θ.","Slope is tan θ, so both lines have the same slope.","That means m₁ = m₂.","Example: y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x − 5 are parallel."],"others":"m₁m₂ = −1 is the condition for perpendicular lines. m₁ = −m₂ describes lines that mirror each other about an axis. m₁ + m₂ = 1 has no geometric meaning here.","trap":"Parallel: equal slopes. Perpendicular: product −1.","answer":"m₁ = m₂"},"mth-11-02-m06":{"idea":"Two lines at a right angle have slopes whose product is −1. One slope is the negative reciprocal of the other.","steps":["If one line makes angle θ, the perpendicular line makes angle θ + 90°.","tan(θ + 90°) = −cot θ = −1/tan θ.","So m₂ = −1/m₁, which means m₁m₂ = −1.","Example: slope 2 and slope −1/2 give a product of −1."],"others":"m₁ = m₂ is the condition for parallel lines. m₁m₂ = 1 would make both slopes the same sign, which cannot be a right angle. m₁ + m₂ = 0 gives lines that mirror each other, not lines at 90°.","trap":"A vertical and a horizontal line are perpendicular, but the rule does not apply because a vertical slope is not defined.","answer":"m₁m₂ = −1"},"mth-11-02-m07":{"idea":"When you know one point and the slope, the equation of the line can be written at once. This is the point-slope form.","steps":["For any point (x, y) on the line, the slope to (x₁, y₁) is m.","So (y − y₁)/(x − x₁) = m.","Multiply by (x − x₁): y − y₁ = m(x − x₁).","Example: point (2, 3), slope 4 gives y − 3 = 4(x − 2), so y = 4x − 5."],"others":"y = mx − x₁y₁ is not a valid form. The version with plus signs would pass through (−x₁, −y₁). The version with division by m has the slope turned upside down.","trap":"Check by putting (x₁, y₁) in the equation: both sides must be zero.","answer":"y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)"},"mth-11-02-m08":{"idea":"The slope-intercept form shows the slope and the y-intercept at a glance.","steps":["The line cuts the y axis at (0, c ), so c is the y-intercept.","Moving 1 step along x raises y by m.","So y = mx + c.","Check: put x = 0, which gives y = c."],"others":"x = my + c has x and y swapped, so m would be the reciprocal slope. y = m + cx and y = cx + m use c as the slope and m as the intercept.","trap":"m goes with x, and c is the constant.","answer":"y = mx + c"},"mth-11-02-m09":{"idea":"The intercept form uses the two points where the line cuts the axes.","steps":["The line passes through (a, 0) and (0, b ).","Put (a, 0) in x/a + y/b: 1 + 0 = 1.","Put (0, b ): 0 + 1 = 1.","So the line is x/a + y/b = 1."],"others":"ax + by = 1 would give the intercepts 1/a and 1/b. x/b + y/a = 1 has a and b swapped. x/a − y/b = 1 has a wrong sign, unless b is negative.","trap":"Intercepts go in the denominators, with their own signs.","answer":"x/a + y/b = 1"},"mth-11-02-m10":{"idea":"To find the intercepts of a line, bring the equation to the form x/a + y/b = 1.","steps":["Divide 4x − 3y = 12 by 12.","This gives x/3 + y/(−4) = 1.","So the x-intercept is 3 and the y-intercept is −4.","Check: y = 0 gives x = 3, and x = 0 gives y = −4."],"others":"12 and −12 are the right-hand side value, not the intercepts. −3 and 4 are the intercepts of a different line, with 4x − 3y = −12. 4 and −3 are the coefficients, not the intercepts.","trap":"The coefficients of x and y are not the intercepts.","answer":"3 and −4"},"mth-11-02-m11":{"idea":"To read the slope from a general equation, solve it for y.","steps":["Start with ax + by + c = 0 (b not zero).","Move terms: by = −ax − c.","Divide by b: y = −(a/b )x − c/b.","Comparing with y = mx + c gives slope m = −a/b."],"others":"b/a and −b/a use the coefficients the wrong way round. a/b has the sign wrong. Check with 3x + 4y − 5 = 0: the slope is −3/4.","trap":"The slope is minus the x coefficient over the y coefficient.","answer":"−a/b"},"mth-11-02-m12":{"idea":"The distance of a point from a line means the shortest distance. That is the straight drop from the point down to the line, meeting it at a right angle.","steps":["Put the point in the left side of the equation: ax₁ + by₁ + c.","Take the absolute value, because distance is never negative.","Divide by √(a² + b²), the length of the vector (a, b ).","So d = |ax₁ + by₁ + c|/√(a² + b²)."],"others":"A fraction without absolute value could be negative. Dividing by a² + b² or by a + b gives wrong units and wrong values. A square root on the top does not appear in the formula.","trap":"The square root belongs below, and the absolute value above.","answer":"|ax₁ + by₁ + c|/√(a² + b²)"},"mth-11-02-m13":{"idea":"The distance between two parallel lines is the distance of any point of one line from the other line.","steps":["Both lines must have the same a and b.","Take a point (x₁, y₁) on the first line, so ax₁ + by₁ = −c₁.","Its distance from the second line is |−c₁ + c₂|/√(a² + b²).","So d = |c₁ − c₂|/√(a² + b²)."],"others":"No square root in the denominator gives the wrong size. Dividing by a + b is not the length of the normal. |c₁ + c₂| is a sum, but the lines differ by the difference of their constants.","trap":"Example: 3x + 4y = 5 and 3x + 4y = 15 give 10/5 = 2.","answer":"|c₁ − c₂|/√(a² + b²)"},"mth-11-02-m14":{"idea":"We use the point-to-line distance formula and put in the numbers.","steps":["d = |ax₁ + by₁ + c|/√(a² + b²), with a = 3, b = 4, c = −5.","Numerator: |3(3) + 4(4) − 5| = |9 + 16 − 5| = 20.","Denominator: √(9 + 16) = 5.","d = 20/5 = 4."],"others":"20 is the numerator, with the division left out. 5 is the denominator alone. 2 is the distance between the parallel lines 3x + 4y = 5 and 3x + 4y = 15.","trap":"Do not forget to divide by √(a² + b²).","answer":"4"},"mth-11-02-m15":{"idea":"The angle between two lines is the difference of their inclinations. The tangent of a difference gives the formula.","steps":["Let m₁ = tan θ₁ and m₂ = tan θ₂.","tan(θ₁ − θ₂) = (tan θ₁ − tan θ₂)/(1 + tan θ₁ tan θ₂).","So tan φ = (m₁ − m₂)/(1 + m₁m₂).","Take the absolute value to get the acute angle between the lines."],"others":"|m₁ − m₂| alone leaves out the denominator. The two forms with a minus in the denominator or a sum on top are the formulas for tan(θ₁ + θ₂) type of terms, not for the angle between lines.","trap":"The denominator is 1 + m₁m₂. When it is zero, the lines are perpendicular.","answer":"tan φ = |(m₁ − m₂)/(1 + m₁m₂)|"},"mth-11-02-m16":{"idea":"We find the angle from the two slopes using the angle formula.","steps":["The slopes are m₁ = 2 and m₂ = 1/3.","tan φ = |(m₁ − m₂)/(1 + m₁m₂)|.","Top: 2 − 1/3 = 5/3. Bottom: 1 + 2/3 = 5/3.","tan φ = 1, so φ = 45°."],"others":"60° would need tan φ = √3 and 30° would need tan φ = 1/√3. 90° would need the denominator to be zero, but it is 5/3.","trap":"Check: tan⁻¹ 2 = 63.43° and tan⁻¹(1/3) = 18.43°. The difference is 45°.","answer":"45°"},"mth-11-02-m17":{"idea":"The normal form describes a line by its distance from the origin and the direction of the perpendicular to it.","steps":["Draw the perpendicular from the origin to the line. Its length is p.","It makes an angle α with the positive x axis.","Every point (x, y) of the line has x cos α + y sin α = p.","So p is the distance from the origin."],"others":"The version with a minus sign between the terms and a y-intercept is not the normal form. The one with sine and cosine swapped does not give a distance. The form with divisions by cos α and sin α is not standard.","trap":"In the normal form, p is always positive.","answer":"x cos α + y sin α = p, where p is the distance of the line from the origin"},"mth-11-02-m18":{"idea":"To get the normal form, divide the equation by √(a² + b²). The constant on the right is then the distance p.","steps":["For 3x + 4y = 10, a = 3 and b = 4.","√(3² + 4²) = 5.","Divide by 5: (3/5)x + (4/5)y = 2.","So cos α = 3/5, sin α = 4/5 and p = 2."],"others":"10/3 comes from dividing by only the x coefficient. 5 is the divisor, not the distance. 10 is the constant before dividing.","trap":"Check: the distance from the origin is |−10|/5 = 2.","answer":"2"},"mth-11-02-m19":{"idea":"A line parallel to the x axis is a horizontal line. All its points have the same y value.","steps":["A horizontal line has slope 0.","Every point on it has the same y, so y = k.","So y = 5 is parallel to the x axis.","x = 5 is vertical, and the other two have a non-zero slope."],"others":"x = 5 is parallel to the y axis. x + y = 5 has slope −1. y = 5x has slope 5.","trap":"y = constant is horizontal. x = constant is vertical.","answer":"y = 5"},"mth-11-02-m20":{"idea":"The meeting point of two lines satisfies both equations. We solve them together.","steps":["From x − y = 1, we get x = y + 1.","Put this in 2x + 3y = 12: 2(y + 1) + 3y = 12.","So 5y + 2 = 12, which gives y = 2.","Then x = 3, so the point is (3, 2)."],"others":"(2, 3) has the coordinates swapped. (3, 3) gives 2(3) + 3(3) = 15, not 12. (1, 0) satisfies x − y = 1 but gives 2 for the first line.","trap":"Check: both equations must be true at the point.","answer":"(3, 2)"},"mth-11-02-m21":{"idea":"Three points are collinear when the slope between any two of them is the same.","steps":["Slope from (1, 2) to (5, 8): (8 − 2)/(5 − 1) = 6/4 = 3/2.","Slope from (1, 2) to (3, k): (k − 2)/(3 − 1) = (k − 2)/2.","Equate: (k − 2)/2 = 3/2.","So k − 2 = 3 and k = 5."],"others":"6 would need a slope of 2, 4 would need a slope of 1, and 3 would need a slope of 1/2. None of them match 3/2.","trap":"The middle point lies on the line, so its y must be 5.","answer":"5"},"mth-11-02-m22":{"idea":"The area of a triangle can be found from its three corner points with a determinant style formula.","steps":["Area = (1/2)|x₁(y₂ − y₃) + x₂(y₃ − y₁) + x₃(y₁ − y₂)|.","Put in the points: 1(2 − 6) + 5(6 − 1) + 3(1 − 2).","This is −4 + 25 − 3 = 18.","Area = (1/2) × 18 = 9 square units."],"others":"18 is the value before taking half. 12 and 4.5 do not match any of the arithmetic.","trap":"Do not forget the 1/2 and the absolute value.","answer":"9 square units"},"mth-11-02-n01":{"idea":"The slope is rise over run, found from two points.","steps":["m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁).","Put in (1, 2) and (4, 8): (8 − 2)/(4 − 1).","This is 6/3.","So m = 2.00."],"trap":"Check: tan⁻¹ 2 is 63.43°, a sensible steep angle.","answer":"2.00"},"mth-11-02-n02":{"idea":"We use the distance formula for a point and a line.","steps":["d = |ax₁ + by₁ + c|/√(a² + b²).","Here a = 3, b = 4, c = −5 and the point is (3, 4).","Numerator: |9 + 16 − 5| = 20. Denominator: √25 = 5.","d = 20/5 = 4.000."],"trap":"Do not skip the division by √(a² + b²).","answer":"4.000"},"mth-11-02-n03":{"idea":"The angle between two lines comes from their slopes.","steps":["m₁ = 2 and m₂ = 1/3.","tan φ = |(m₁ − m₂)/(1 + m₁m₂)| = |(5/3)/(5/3)| = 1.","φ = tan⁻¹ 1.","So φ = 45.00°."],"trap":"Check: 63.43° − 18.43° = 45.00°.","answer":"45.00°"},"mth-11-02-n04":{"idea":"To find where two lines meet, solve their equations together. We want only the x coordinate.","steps":["From x − y = 1, x = y + 1.","Put in 2x + 3y = 12: 2(y + 1) + 3y = 12.","5y = 10, so y = 2.","Then x = y + 1 = 3.000."],"trap":"Check: 2(3) + 3(2) = 12 and 3 − 2 = 1.","answer":"3.000"},"mth-11-02-n05":{"idea":"We use the coordinate formula for the area of a triangle.","steps":["Area = (1/2)|x₁(y₂ − y₃) + x₂(y₃ − y₁) + x₃(y₁ − y₂)|.","Put in: 1(2 − 6) + 5(6 − 1) + 3(1 − 2).","This is −4 + 25 − 3 = 18.","Area = 18/2 = 9.00 square units."],"trap":"Do not forget the factor 1/2.","answer":"9.00 square units"},"mth-11-02-n06":{"idea":"A line given by its two intercepts passes through (3, 0) and (0, −4). We find its slope.","steps":["The points are (3, 0) and (0, −4).","m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) = (−4 − 0)/(0 − 3).","This is −4/−3 = 4/3.","So m = 1.333."],"trap":"Check with the equation: x/3 − y/4 = 1 gives 4x − 3y = 12, and the slope is 4/3.","answer":"1.333"},"mth-11-02-n07":{"idea":"For two parallel lines, the distance depends only on the constants and the common a and b.","steps":["The lines are 3x + 4y − 5 = 0 and 3x + 4y − 15 = 0.","d = |c₁ − c₂|/√(a² + b²) = |−5 + 15|/√(9 + 16).","That is 10/5.","So d = 2.000."],"trap":"The coefficients a and b must be equal in both lines before you use this formula.","answer":"2.000"},"mth-11-02-n08":{"idea":"To write a line in normal form, divide by √(a² + b²). The constant is then the distance p from the origin.","steps":["3x + 4y = 10, so a = 3 and b = 4.","√(3² + 4²) = 5.","Divide by 5: (3/5)x + (4/5)y = 2.","Here cos α = 0.6, sin α = 0.8 and p = 2.00."],"trap":"p must be positive, and here 10/5 = 2 is positive.","answer":"2.00"},"mth-11-02-n09":{"idea":"Collinear points lie on one line, so the slope between any two of them is the same.","steps":["The slope from (1, 2) to (5, 8) is 6/4 = 1.5.","The line is y = 2 + 1.5(x − 1).","At x = 3: k = 2 + 1.5 × 2.","So k = 5.000."],"trap":"Check: the triangle (1, 2), (3, 5), (5, 8) has zero area.","answer":"5.000"}};
